WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.144 --> 00:00:04.739
- [Narrator] Americans, we love ice cream.

00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:08.835
Each person eats about
24 quarts of it per year.

00:00:08.835 --> 00:00:10.477
And that's for one big reason.

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:11.977
It tastes amazing.

00:00:12.888 --> 00:00:16.486
But, someone had to teach
Ben and Jerry's how to do it.

00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:20.153
Turns out, that guy,
he works at Penn State.

00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:24.875
This is Dr. Bob Roberts--

00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:26.054
Hold on.

00:00:26.054 --> 00:00:28.312
Okay, this is Dr. Bob Roberts.

00:00:28.312 --> 00:00:31.224
He teaches ice cream
courses at Penn State.

00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:32.720
- I do.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.278
I have two courses.

00:00:34.278 --> 00:00:37.848
Ice Cream Short Course and Ice Cream 101.

00:00:37.848 --> 00:00:40.175
- [Narrator] Side note,
these are the type of books

00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:43.056
ice cream professors read.

00:00:43.056 --> 00:00:44.888
Okay, back to the story.

00:00:44.888 --> 00:00:46.328
- [Dr. Roberts] Part of the reason that

00:00:46.328 --> 00:00:47.704
ice cream is so good in the U.S.

00:00:47.704 --> 00:00:51.352
is that we've been teaching
ice cream for 125 years.

00:00:51.352 --> 00:00:54.083
- [Narrator] And Bob, he's
been teaching at Penn State

00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:55.611
for 25 of those.

00:00:55.611 --> 00:00:58.704
And he's had some pretty famous students.

00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:01.603
- Companies like Unilever,
Nestle, Breyer's,

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:04.056
Ben and Jerry's, um, you name the company,

00:01:04.056 --> 00:01:05.880
they've sent people here.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.501
- [Narrator] That's because
the Penn State creamery

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:10.371
is on the forefront of
ice cream technology.

00:01:10.371 --> 00:01:12.083
- [Dr. Roberts] Ice cream
is a formulated food.

00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:13.971
There is no naturally occurring ice cream.

00:01:13.971 --> 00:01:16.291
You have to put the ingredients together

00:01:16.291 --> 00:01:18.883
and process it to make ice cream.

00:01:18.883 --> 00:01:20.451
- [Narrator] Bob and his department,

00:01:20.451 --> 00:01:22.189
they spend their time figuring out

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:24.323
how to make ice cream even better.

00:01:24.323 --> 00:01:26.115
- When we look at studying ice cream,

00:01:26.115 --> 00:01:27.643
we study ice cream from cow to the cone.

00:01:27.643 --> 00:01:29.251
So we look at what happens on the farm,

00:01:29.251 --> 00:01:30.893
what happens with the milk.

00:01:30.893 --> 00:01:32.216
- [Narrator] Wait, there's a farm?

00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:35.779
- [Dr. Roberts] We have
a herd of about 250 or so

00:01:35.779 --> 00:01:38.147
milking Holsteins and
yes they are on campus.

00:01:38.147 --> 00:01:39.469
(cow mooing)

00:01:39.469 --> 00:01:41.869
- [Narrator] So yeah, he knows ice cream.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:44.504
But he says that he's
not an ice cream purist.

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:48.109
- I'm not sure what an
ice cream purist is.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:49.752
But I wouldn't eat frozen yogurt

00:01:49.752 --> 00:01:51.347
if I had the opportunity to eat ice cream.

00:01:51.347 --> 00:01:52.733
(laughing)

00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:54.621
- [Narrator] Yeah, well,
that does make sense

00:01:54.621 --> 00:01:57.427
considering he is the
authority on ice cream.

00:01:57.427 --> 00:01:59.296
Bob, what's your favorite flavor?

00:01:59.296 --> 00:02:01.307
- Hmm, dulce de Leche.

00:02:01.307 --> 00:02:02.512
- [Narrator] Ooh, yeah.

00:02:02.512 --> 00:02:05.679
(light string music)

